.TH FEM 2
.SH NAME
fem: assemble, loadfn, loadconst, dirichlet, newproblem, solve \- finite-element Poisson assembly and solve
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "fem.m";
fem := load Fem Fem->PATH;
Problem: import fem;

(p, err) := fem->newproblem(
	"mesh 20x20x20 domain 1x1x1\en" +
	"material diffusivity 1.0\en" +
	"load 1.0\en" +
	"bc dirichlet 0.0\en" +
	"solver cg tolerance 1e-8");
if(err != nil)
	...
(x, iters, resid, serr) := fem->solve(p);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fem
is a real finite-element assembly on
.IR femesh (2)'s
structured hex8 mesh: trilinear shape functions, 2x2x2 Gauss
quadrature, scattered into one global
.IR sparse (2)
CSR matrix \(em this, not a stencil closure, is what makes it a
finite-\fIelement\fP proxy rather than another
.IR pde (2)
equation. Where
.IR pde (2)'s
implicit solvers apply an operator matrix-free,
.IR krylov (2)'s
.B Apply
for a
.I Fem
problem is a real assembled sparse matrix's own
.BR matvec .
.PP
.B Assemble
builds the global stiffness matrix for constant-diffusivity Poisson
(\-div(D grad u) = f) on a
.IR femesh (2)
.BR Grid ,
with no boundary conditions applied yet.
.B Loadfn
builds the consistent FE load vector for a source
.I src
(a bare function of physical
.RI ( x , y , z ),
evaluated at each real quadrature point, the same convention
.IR krylov (2)'s
own
.B Apply
uses since Limbo has no closure literals);
.B loadconst
is
.B loadfn
for a source constant everywhere, the common case.
.B Dirichlet
eliminates a set of fixed nodes from an assembled
.RI ( a , b )
pair \(em a thin wrapper over
.IR sparse (2)'s
own
.BR dirichlet ,
kept here so a caller never needs to import
.I sparse
just to set a boundary condition.
.SS "The problem-specification language"
.B Newproblem
parses a spec into a ready, fully assembled and boundary-eliminated
.B Problem
in one call, the same relationship a printf format string has to what
it produces \(em the same shape
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.B newproblem
has, layered on
.IR femesh (2)'s
mesh grammar the same way
.IR pde (2)
is layered on
.IR mesh (2)'s.
A spec is one or more newline-separated lines:
.TP
.BI "mesh " NXxNYxNZ " [domain " WxHxD "]"
Delegates to
.IR femesh (2);
see there for the grammar. Required.
.TP
.BI "material diffusivity " D
The constant diffusivity passed to
.BR assemble .
Required.
.TP
.BI "load " Q
A constant volumetric source, passed to
.BR loadconst .
Optional; defaults to 0.0.
.TP
.BI "bc dirichlet " V
Fixes every boundary node (see
.IR femesh (2)'s
.BR isboundary )
to the uniform value
.IR V .
Required \(em an assembled system with no boundary condition at all
is singular.
.TP
.BI "solver gmres|cg [tolerance " T "] [restart " R "] [maxiter " N ]
Which
.IR krylov (2)
method solves the assembled system, and its parameters; see
.IR krylov (2)
for what each means. Unlike
.IR pde (2)
there is no
.B "solver explicit"
here: an assembled system has no stability-limited substep to fall
back to, so solving
.IR Ax=b
for a real sparse
.I A
is always an actual linear solve.
.PP
.B Solve
runs
.IR p . a " x = " p . b
via
.IR krylov (2)
using
.IR p . a 's
own
.B matvec
as the operator \(em a real sparse solve, not a matrix-free one \(em
and returns the nodal solution (indexed exactly like
.IR femesh (2)'s
node ids), the iteration count, and the final residual, or an error
string if the solve failed to converge (in which case the other three
returns should not be trusted).
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/fem.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/fem.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR femesh (2),
.IR sparse (2),
.IR krylov (2),
.IR pde (2)
